title = Forks, merge request from fork, and DVCS workflow
mode = platform_theory

[diagram]

[code]
   upstream project (canonical)
              │
         fork │  (GitLab UI: duplicate namespace)
              ▼
   contributor fork ──► branch push ──► MR → upstream default branch
              │
              └──── optional: shared runners on forks (policy)
[/code]

[center][color=#fca326]“Allow commits from upstream members”[/color]  ·  maintainer pushes to fork branch[/center]

[code]
   git remote add upstream git@gitlab.com:group/upstream.git
   git fetch upstream && git rebase upstream/main
[/code]

[description]

[b]Forking on GitLab[/b] duplicates the repository into another namespace under your account or group so you can [code]git push[/code] without Maintainer access on the canonical project—then open a merge request targeting upstream [code]main[/code], the same integration pattern Oh My Git teaches with remotes, fetch, and merge/rebase before asking maintainers to integrate your work.

[b]Merge request from fork:[/b] reviewers see diff, pipelines, and threads in the upstream project UI while commits live on fork refs until merge. Sync fork when upstream advances: fetch upstream, rebase or merge default into your branch, push to fork, MR updates automatically.

[b]Pipeline trust on forks:[/b] merge request pipelines may run with reduced secrets—instance admins often disable shared runners for external forks to prevent crypto-mining or secret exfiltration via malicious [code].gitlab-ci.yml[/code] changes. Expect slower feedback for drive-by contributors; document how to run tests locally.

[b]Allow commits from upstream members:[/b] lets maintainers push fixes directly to contributor fork branches when rebases are messy—speeds collaboration without forcing MR authors to grant full repo access. Audit who can push; use branch protection on fork default branch anyway.

[b]Internal forking:[/b] enterprises fork inside the same top-level group for compliance boundaries—Git mechanics match public OSS, only ACLs differ. Subscriptions and CI billing may still treat forks as separate projects.

[b]Fork cleanup:[/b] stale forks accumulate storage; archive after merge. Rename upstream project and update clone URLs on all active forks.

[b]Compared to same-repo branching:[/b] many internal teams skip forks and use protected [code]main[/code] plus MR-only feature branches—forks remain essential for external contributors without write access.

[b]Confidential issues and forks:[/b] confidential upstream issues must not leak through fork MR descriptions or pipeline logs—review MR templates and CI output scrubbing when security-sensitive work flows through public fork pipelines with reduced secrets.

[b]Credit and attribution:[/b] squash merge on upstream may collapse contributor authorship metadata—configure squash settings to preserve co-author trailers where community norms expect attribution across fork boundaries.

[b]When things go wrong:[/b] MR pipeline pending forever on fork often means shared runners disabled for external contributors—maintainer must trigger pipeline or provide fork runner. “Permission denied” pushing to upstream is correct—retarget push to fork [code]origin[/code]. Huge MR conflicts after long-lived fork means painful rebase—sync early and often. Maintainer push to fork branch without comment confuses authors who rebased locally on stale tips.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] treat fork MRs as partially trusted code execution: review diffs, cap CI secrets, and audit runner usage the same way you would for any external patch series.
